## What this crate is

A value of a declared signature: one member of the format's representable set, held alone in the
carrier the placement derives at sole occupancy. `arvo-format` says which values exist and how an exact
result is put back onto them; `arvo-placement` says how many bits one of them takes when it sits alone.
Neither holds a number. This crate does, and holds nothing else.

Every operation on a value is defined as the format's own operation on its slot, so the value never
states a behaviour of its own. Its sum is the oracle's sum, `add::<S>` in `arvo-format`, and the
contract is an equality: the slot of `a.add(b)` is `add::<S>(a.slot(), b.slot(), Dither::UNUSED)` for
every pair of members of every admitted signature. The difference, the negation and the product stand
on the same contract against the format's `sub`, `neg` and `mul`, and the quotient and the conversion
against its `div` and `convert`. The carrier arithmetic is the
mechanism that meets that equality fast, and it is free to be anything that does.

It depends on `arvo-format` and `arvo-placement`, and on `notko` for the `Maybe` the format answers in
where it has nothing to name. It adds nothing to the placement. Of the format it reads three things
that were added there for it, each the format's own to state: which member is one,
`multiplicative_identity`, a signed fixed-point point, `Fixed`, and the two aliases that replace one
coordinate of a signature, `WithOverflow` and `WithRounding`. `no_std`, no allocation, every operation
a `const fn`. As it builds by default it has no unstable feature and no dependency beyond those three.
Its cargo features are the mutants its own tests turn on and two that are purely opt in, `hlist` and
`hlist-const`, which make a value the count of a type-level list and are described under the count
below. The second of them enables the one unstable feature this crate can enable.

## What a value signature admits

`Sole` is the bound a value is generic over. It is implemented once, over every `DeclaredSignature`
whose format's slot family has a row in `arvo_placement::SoleCarrier`, so code generic over a value's
signature bounds it by `Sole` and nothing more, and no signature with a row is left out of it.

What the carrier arithmetic needs beyond the row is checked rather than listed. `Sole::ADMITTED` asks
the signature what it already declares, and every door forces it, so no value of a signature that fails
it is built:

1. The slot family is admitted by its own crate, `Slots::ADMITTED`, and its row names the container the
   placement derives. That is asserted here as `carrier_agrees`, not read through the row's own
   `SoleCarrier::ADMITTED`: the constant is provided, an outside row may replace it with one that checks
   nothing, and a row naming the 8-bit container for a 16-bit family would then have its sums truncated
   by the 8-bit rung.
2. The slot range is the whole signed or the whole unsigned range of its width, which is what the
   carrier arithmetic reduces onto.
3. The overflow policy declares wrapping or saturation, read from `Overflow::POLICY`.
4. The rounding mode reads no dither, read from `Rounding::MODE`.
5. The format's quantum is constant and its phase is zero. That is where the sum of two members is the
   member at the sum of their slots, which is what lets the carrier add stand in for the format's
   addition.

So the inventory stays open, as `ruling::the_format_spine_is_canon` has it: a slot family, a policy or a
mode of another crate is held on exactly the terms the shipped ones are, by declaring what passes the
check, and nothing here names the instances that may. Integers, fixed point at any constant exponent and
scaled integers at any radix pass. `HalfUp` is the format's `half_up`, a tie toward positive infinity at
every sign, and not the `HALF_UP` of Java or Python: a tie at -2.5 goes to -2.

What is refused, and why, so that each refusal reads as a decision rather than a gap:

| Refused | Where it fails | Why |
|---|---|---|
| A policy that clamps, `Clamp` among them | `Sole::ADMITTED` | With no bound to carry, clamping is saturation under another name. |
| A mode that reads a dither, `Stochastic` among them | `Sole::ADMITTED` | A value has nowhere to carry a dither. |
| A slot range that is part of its width's range, or offset | `Sole::ADMITTED` | The carrier arithmetic reduces onto the whole range. |
| A phase, an indexed quantum | `Sole::ADMITTED` | The sum of two slots is not the slot of the sum. |
| Width 0, width past 64 | `SoleCarrier` bound | No ladder container holds the family. |

The refusals by `ADMITTED` are failed constants, reported at the consumer's line, where a sealed bound
would have been a missing impl. That is the price of an open admission, and `arvo-format`'s own `Slots`
pays it the same way.

The one seal kept is on the machine integers. `Machine` is sealed to the host's plain integers of 8 to
64 bits and the two pointer-width ones, because the bit copy between two of them is sound only where
every bit pattern is a valid value. These are what the host offers, not instances of a contract the
canon opens, so the open inventory does not reach them; `Held`, the map from a ladder container to the
machine integer of its size, is private for the same reason. Both seals are what
`ruling::behaviour_is_stated_per_declared_signature_and_the_premise_dissolves` asks of the carrier: it
is observable through the host's layout and through nothing the value declares, so no consumer names
the integer that holds a value or adds one the bit copy would trust.

## How the value holds its carrier

`Num<S: Sole, C: DebugCheck = Abort>` is a `repr(transparent)` newtype over one machine integer, its
field private. Which ladder container the value sits in is the placement's answer, read from
`arvo_placement::SoleCarrier` and not restated here. What this crate adds is the host integer of that
container's size, through `Held`, one row per container, and it is always the unsigned one: the sign is
the slot family's, read from its range where an operation dispatches. The value's size is the
container's, which the placement checks against `derive_sole` at every row.

The carrier is the placement's output and not a coordinate of the signature, so no consumer can name
the integer that holds it. `Held` and `Machine` sit in a module the crate does not export. `Sole`
declares no associated type and reaches them only through a bound in its own supertrait list,
`DeclaredSignature<Format: Format<Slots: SoleCarrier<Carrier: Held>>>`, which the compiler extends to
every place `S: Sole` holds. So `Num<S>` is well formed in any code generic over `S: Sole`, while a
consumer that tries `S::Repr`, a projection on `Sole`, or the map by its path is refused at build. The
container is the placement's public answer, and the carrier's size stays observable through the host's
layout and nothing the value declares.

What the value stores, and how, is this crate's choice under a question still open.
`ruling::a_format_datum_is_one_point_and_a_saturating_end_is_one_point` settles that the
value holds one point of the format's domain, and that a type holds it at all is what the two rows
named first in the canon list presuppose. Neither settles that what is stored is the member's
slot, that one member has one encoding, or that the container a width takes is fixed once a release
has shipped. Those three stay open under `question::what_a_datum_stands_for`, and this crate takes
them as its own: it stores the slot, keeps one bit pattern per member so equality is the carrier's, and
reads its container from the placement, whose layout a consumer can observe and so can come to rely on.
A later ruling that answers otherwise changes this section and the code beneath it.

A `const fn` cannot call a trait method on the carrier. So each operation is written once: it moves the
bits to the machine integer of the same size, signed exactly where the slot family is, calls that rung's
function, and moves the result back. The match it dispatches on is over constants of the instantiation,
the carrier's size and `Sole::SIGNED`, so one arm survives monomorphisation and the backend sees one
call. The move is a bit copy and the crate's one `unsafe`. It is bounded on `Machine`, where every bit
pattern is a valid value, and guarded by an assertion that the two sizes are equal; that assertion is an
ordinary one rather than an inline `const`, because the dead arms are monomorphised as well and would
fail it, and on the arm taken it holds and folds away.

The rung functions are written once per carrier width and expanded by one macro. Below the carrier's
width two members sum without leaving the carrier, so `add` is the machine's wrapping add followed by
the adaptation onto the declared width. At the carrier's width the machine's own wrapping or saturating
add is already the answer.

The signed difference and the signed negation go the same way: the machine's wrapping operation and
the adaptation below the carrier's width, and the machine's own wrapping or saturating operation at it.
The unsigned ones part from that under saturation. A difference or a negation below zero leaves the
carrier as a wrapped bit pattern, and the unsigned adaptation would read that pattern as a large
number and pin it to the top, where the format completes a position below the range to its lowest
member. So the unsigned saturating difference is the machine's `saturating_sub`, the unsigned
saturating negation is zero, and neither passes through the adaptation. Under wrapping both are the
machine's wrapping operation and the mask.

The product, where it is carrier arithmetic at all, which `## The operations` settles, needs no case
on the width. Under wrapping it is the machine's wrapping product and the adaptation, since the low
bits of a product depend only on the low bits of its factors. Under saturation it is the machine's
checked product: where that fits the machine integer the adaptation completes it, and where it does
not the result is the end of the range on the product's sign, the top for an unsigned one.

## Where the host integers are named

This crate is where the containers the placement derives meet the host's machine integers, so it is
declared beside `arvo-format` and `arvo-bits` as introducing the numeric category, and the
bare-primitive lints read that declaration as their scope. The declaration covers a named surface and
nothing else:

1. `Held`, its rows from the four ladder containers to the unsigned machine integers of their sizes,
   `Machine` with the bit copy between two of them, and `Num`'s private field. None of these can be
   named outside the crate.
2. The rung arithmetic, written once per carrier width, and the dispatch onto it by the carrier's size
   and the slot family's sign. Private as well.
3. The doors to the host: `from_host` and `host` on `USize` and `ISize`, and `from_index`,
   `holds_index` and `index` on every value, with `HostInteger`, the sealed trait over the host
   integers those three take and give.
4. The slot index, a bare `i128` in `arvo-format`'s `Slot`, where `constant` takes it as a const
   parameter, the value is built from one, and `Hash` hashes it.
5. The signatures core fixes for the traits `Num` implements, `PartialEq::eq` returning `bool` among
   them, and the crate's own tests.

Everything else is written in the stack's own types: `Bool` for every predicate, `Width` and `Policy`
from the signature, `Ordering` for the order, and `Num` itself at every position a consumer sees.

## The debug check

Whether a debug build aborts when a result leaves the range is an axis of its own, and it is the
consumer's. `Num` takes it as its second parameter, a `DebugCheck`, defaulting to `Abort`: under
`Abort` a result that leaves the range aborts in a debug build, in `add` and in every door that adapts,
and under `Quiet` no build aborts and the completion the overflow policy gives is what the operation
means. `DebugCheck` is open, one associated constant, `ABORTS`, so a check of another crate is one more
marker.

It is no property of the overflow policy. The adaptation is total and a panic diverges, so the panic is
not one of the things a policy may be; it is how an adaptation is applied under a build profile, and
`arvo-format`'s design states that from both directions. `Wrap` and `Saturate` therefore state the
adaptation and only that, and there is no second spelling of either.
`ruling::the_overflow_panic_is_permitted_and_bounded` makes the panic the go-to on debug builds for
what typestate cannot catch, and was ratified deliberately loose. The default is that direction, and
the parameter is what keeps it a default rather than a rule written over the consumer.

The vehicle is a type parameter with a default rather than a cargo feature, for two reasons the sketch
`202611130550-m67x_the-debug-check-axis` builds and runs. A feature is unified across the build: two
crates that use one signature, one of which turns the feature on, get the second crate's behaviour
changed under a signature it never touched; in the sketch's run a crate that names no feature, and
so asks for the abort, has its sum complete once another crate in the build turns the feature on.
A type parameter keeps one type one behaviour, since two choices are two types. And a consumer with
no feature line reaches the parameter, while a feature is reached only by editing a manifest. The
default keeps `Num<S>` meaning what it meant.

The assertion never lives inside the total operation. It asks a verdict, `fits_add`, which says whether
the exact sum of two members is a member, and only where `C::ABORTS` holds and `debug_assertions` is on;
in a release build, and under `Quiet` in every build, the branch is a constant false and clears at
lowering, as `ruling::never_a_runtime_check_and_one_lowered_path` asks. `sub`, `neg` and `mul` ask
`fits_sub`, `fits_neg` and `fits_mul` the same way, under the same two conditions. The first two read
the slots' difference and negation against the range. `fits_mul` asks `arvo_format::panic_on_overflow`
of `product_position`, so at a fractional exponent it is a verdict on the rounded slot and cannot
disagree with what `mul` returns. `div` and `convert` ask `fits_div` and `fits_convert`, which ask the
same of `quotient_position` and `converted_position`. The doors that adapt ask
`arvo_format::panic_on_overflow` of the position they adapt, the same verdict the format ships, and
`from_index` asks `holds_index`, which is that verdict for a position on the grid. The accumulator's
`finish` is a conversion and asks as one. The
check changes neither the member nor its carrier: two values of one signature under two checks hold the
same member in the same bits.

## The doors

Every door is a `const fn` and every one is total or refused at build. None checks at run time.

`Num::constant::<V>()` takes a slot index as a bare `i128` const parameter and is refused at build, as
a failed constant, where `V` is not a member. `Num::adapted(exact)` ingests an `arvo_format::Exact`
through the format's own `adapt`, so a position between two slots is rounded by the signature's mode and
a position outside the range is completed by its policy.

`Num::slot` is the unwrap door and says so: it gives the member's `arvo_format::Slot`, the coordinate
the signature declares. No door takes or gives the carrier. A value's bits come in by the declared
adaptation from an exact point, or as a member by `constant`, and go out as the slot, so every door is a
function of the declared signature and none of the placement.

`USize` and `ISize` carry two more, `from_host` and `host`, taking and giving the host's `usize` and
`isize`. Their point is the platform-width member of the format family, so the host's integer is that
width's own encoding and every host value is a member: both are bit copies, and neither adapts.

Every value has a door for a host integer as well, stated over the slot index. A host integer names an
index: for an integer point the integer itself, and for a fixed-point one the stored integer, the count
of quanta. `HostInteger` is the sealed trait over the integers the door takes, the host's eight machine
integers of 8 to 64 bits and its two pointer-width ones.

1. `Num::from_index::<H>(host)` is the member at that index, completed by the declared policy where the
   index is outside the range. It equals `Num::adapted(Exact::on_grid(Slot::at(host)))` for every host
   value, and under `Abort` a debug build aborts exactly where that would.
2. `Num::holds_index::<H>(host)` returns `Bool`, whether the index is a member. It is the verdict
   `from_index` asks.
3. `index::<H>()` gives the member's slot index as an `H`. It is a failed constant where the range is
   not inside `H`'s, so it never truncates: a signed 8-bit integer gives an `i8` and is refused a `u8`.

The carrier is not named by any of the three. A 13-bit unsigned integer gives its index as a `u16`, a
`u32` or an `i64`, whichever the consumer asks for and the range fits, and takes one from any of the
ten.

This door exists because `adapted` runs the format's exact step, four limbs wide, and a number the
program computes should not pay that to become a value. Nothing rounds on the way in, since an index
is on the grid, so the door needs only the completion, and that is carrier-width arithmetic. It is
done at 64 bits and no wider. The host integer is extended to 64 bits by its own sign. Under wrapping
the low bits of the declared width are kept and read by the range's sign. Under saturation the value
is compared against the two ends, and the two cases in which the host's sign and the range's differ
are answered before any comparison: a negative host into an unsigned range is the bottom, and an
unsigned host above the signed 64-bit maximum into a signed range is the top. Every quantity but the
host value is a constant of the instantiation.

## The operations

`add` is the sum, total, and equal to the oracle on every pair. `fits_add` is its verdict, returning
`Bool`.

`sub` is the difference and `neg` the negation, total, each equal to the format's `sub` and `neg` on
every pair and every member, with `fits_sub` and `fits_neg` their verdicts. At a zero phase the
difference of two members sits at the difference of their slots and the negation at the negated slot,
both on the grid, so both are carrier arithmetic and nothing rounds. The negation of an unsigned
member is admitted because the format admits it: its position is below the range unless the member is
zero, and the adaptation puts it back, as the two's complement negation at the declared width under
wrapping and as zero under saturation. The value states no behaviour of its own, so it does not refuse
what the format computes.

`mul` is the product, total, equal to the format's `mul` on every pair, with `fits_mul` its verdict.
At a zero phase the product of the members at slots `a` and `b` sits at `a b r^e`, with `r` the radix
and `e` the quantum's exponent, and what that asks of the value depends on `e` alone:

1. At `e` zero the position is the slots' product, on the grid, and only the completion acts. The
   product is carrier arithmetic, the rung's. Every integer takes this arm, the platform-width ones
   among them.
2. At any other `e` the position is the slots' product moved up by `r^e`, or divided by `r^-e` with a
   remainder that the rounding mode decides. The product is `arvo_format::mul` on the two slots,
   called as it stands. The rounding has one home, the format's applied map, and a second one written
   here to be quick would be a second arithmetic kept equal to the first by tests alone.

Which arm an instantiation takes is a constant of it, read from the quantum's exponent, so one arm
survives monomorphisation. The second arm runs the format's exact step, which is formed four 64-bit
limbs wide, and is slow for it. The cure is the format's to ship, an adaptation computed at the
container's width that this crate calls with the machine's widening product, and until it exists the
call carries a `FIXME` naming its ticket. The quotient and the conversion below go through the same
step under the same marker, and for the same reason: the rounding of a ratio has one home.

`mul` forces the format's multiplication obligation as a constant of the instantiation, in both arms.
So a product the format does not admit is refused at build with the format's own message, and the
unsigned 64-bit integer is among them today: `arvo-format` does not yet admit the product over that
range at exponents at or above zero, because the top corner is past its slot index. The carrier arm
would compute a product there, and nothing exists to call it right against, so it does not ship ahead
of the format. When the format admits the range the value's product is admitted with it and nothing
here is edited. The sum, the difference and the negation do not depend on it.

`div` is the quotient, total, equal to the format's `div` on every member and every divisor, with
`fits_div` its verdict. The divisor is `Divisor`, the format's own type, re-exported, taken over the
value's format. That it is not zero is the format's guarantee, carried by the type, and nothing here
states it a second time. A divisor is a constant today, `Divisor::constant::<B>()`. The format's
other constructor takes a run-time slot only over a format in which no slot names the zero, and every
format a value is admitted over has the zero in its range, so a value cannot yet be divided by a
value. The membership type for the non-zero slots is the format's to ship, and the value takes it when
it lands. The format ships no remainder as an operation either, so there is none here: a remainder
defined in this crate would be a behaviour the value states on its own.

`convert::<T>()` takes a value of `S` to a value of any `T: Sole`, with the debug check kept. The
member is placed on `T`'s grid by `T`'s rounding and completed by `T`'s policy, which is the format's
`convert` from `S`'s format onto `T`, and the contract is equality with it on every member.
`fits_convert::<T>()` is its verdict. Where the two formats are one, `convert` gives what `under`
gives, and `under` stays the door for that case because it moves the carrier and computes nothing.

`eq` returns `Bool`, and two values are equal exactly when their slots are, because the carrier is
canonical, one member to one bit pattern, which is the choice `## How the value holds its carrier` names. `cmp` returns `core::cmp::Ordering`; `lt`, `le`, `gt` and `ge`
return `Bool`; `min` and `max` return the value. At a zero phase and a constant quantum the map from slot
to member is monotone, and the rung the dispatch takes is signed exactly where the slot family is, so the
rung's order is the members' order and these read the carrier through it.

`Num` implements `Clone`, `Copy`, `PartialEq` and `Eq` over the carrier, which is canonical, and `Hash`
and `Debug` over the slot: `Hash` hashes the slot's index and `Debug` prints the slot, so neither
depends on the carrier. Each is written by hand so that neither `S` nor `C`
is bounded beyond what the value needs. It implements no
operator trait beyond equality, no ordering trait, no `Default` and no `From` of a host integer. An
operator trait cannot be called in a `const fn`, so the operations are the inherent ones above. The
ordering traits are left out because core's `partial_cmp` answers in a bare `Option`, which this
workspace refuses in every crate that does not introduce fallibility, and `Ord` cannot be had without
it; the order is the inherent `cmp`, which a consumer passes wherever a comparison is taken. A default
would be a policy decided for the consumer. The doors in are `constant`, `adapted` and `from_index`,
each stated over the declared signature and named for what it does. A `From` of a host integer would
complete by the policy under a trait whose name says nothing of it, so the host integer comes in by
the door that says so.

## Identities

`Identity<M>` has one associated constant, `IDENTITY`, and `Additive` and `Multiplicative` are the two
`M` this crate declares. The call shape is `<Num<S> as Identity<Additive>>::IDENTITY`.

`Num<S, C>` implements it for every `S` and `C`. The constant asserts `arvo_format::has_additive_identity` of the
format before anything else, so on a format without one the refusal is a failed constant naming the
missing identity rather than a missing impl. Within what `Sole` admits zero is always slot zero and a
member, so the refusal fires only where the admission would refuse as well, and fires first.

The identity is computed from the format's own predicate rather than declared, so it is not an author's
marker that nothing checks. Associativity stays the verdict it is, `arvo_format::addition_is_associative`,
and no marker for it is declared here.

`Identity<Multiplicative>` is implemented for every `S` and `C` as well, and its constant is the member
`arvo_format::multiplicative_identity` names for the format. Whether some member of a format is one is
the format's question: it reads the phase, the radix and the exponent together and is checked against
the product's own step, so it is answered there and read here. Where the format answers nothing the
refusal is a failed constant naming the missing identity, following
`proposal::a_missing_identity_is_refused_as_a_failed_constant`, which is at one expert and on a
provisional stamp. Within what `Sole` admits that is every format whose exponent is above zero, where
no slot is one, and every format too narrow to reach it, a one-bit signed integer among them, whose
members are zero and minus one. A fixed-point format at exponent `-k` has it at slot `2^k` where the
range reaches that far. The identity does not force the multiplication obligation, so an unsigned
64-bit integer has its one although its product is not admitted yet.

## A value as a count

`notko-hlist` is the workspace's type-level list, and it counts a list's cells in a number type of the
consumer's own through `Cardinal`, a trait of a zero and a successor. Under the feature `hlist`,
`Num<S, C>` implements `Cardinal` for every `S` and `C`, so a list's length and a position's index
land in a value. The impl is here because the orphan rule leaves it to the list's crate or the
value's, and the list has no dependency.

Zero is the additive identity and the successor of a count is its sum with the multiplicative
identity. So a value counts where its format has a one, in whole members: an integer by its slots, a
fixed-point value at exponent `-k` by `2^k` slots. A format without a one has no count, and a length
in it is refused by the identity's own failed constant with the identity's own message. The impl is
not narrowed to the platform-sized integer or to the unsigned ranges, since which type a list is
counted in is the consumer's to say at the bound.

The successor is `add`, so a count past the range is completed by the declared overflow policy, and
under the `Abort` check a debug build aborts on it. Where a length is read as a constant the abort
happens while the build evaluates it, so under `Abort` a list longer than its count type holds does not
build.

There are two paths, as the list has two. `hlist` takes the list without its `const` feature and
implements the trait plainly: nothing in the build is unstable, and a length is a call. `hlist-const`
turns the list's `const` feature on, which makes `Cardinal` a const trait, implements it with a const
impl, and enables `const_trait_impl` in this crate for that one impl: a length is a constant. The
const path exists because a plain impl of a const trait gives no constant, and the constant is what a
consumer indexing by a list's length wants.

Both features are off by default and nothing in this workspace turns either on. The gate is in no
public signature. A consumer bounds on the list's own traits and reads a length, as a call or as a
constant, with no feature attribute of its own, on either path; the const call is made inside the
list's impl. No trait this crate declares becomes a const trait, and the value's operations stay
inherent `const fn`s.

## A value under a sibling signature

Two signatures over one format hold the same members, and differ in what happens to a result that is
not one. `under::<T>()` takes a value of `S` to the value of `T` holding the same member, for any
`T: Sole` whose format is `S`'s. The debug check is kept, being no part of the signature.

The contract is that the slot is unchanged: `a.under::<T>().slot()` is `a.slot()`, the result equals
`Num::adapted(Exact::on_grid(a.slot()))` built under `T`, and crossing back gives `a`. What changes is
every operation after it, which is `T`'s.

The carrier is handed over as it is. The carrier type is derived from the format's slot family and the
encoding is one member to one bit pattern, both functions of the format alone, so the two values are
the same bits in the same machine integer and the body has no arithmetic in it. The compiler holds the
two carrier types equal from the equality of the formats in the bound, with nothing asserted by hand.
Nothing is read of the range, so the debug check has nothing to ask of a crossing.

The caller writes `T: Sole`. `S` being admitted says nothing of `T`, following
`ruling::configuration_is_not_composition_and_a_predicate_never_transports_for_free`, and a target the
admission refuses is refused where the crossing names it, with the admission's own message. A target
over another format is a type error at the call: its slot would have to be rounded or completed onto
the other grid, which is a conversion and not this.

`with_overflow::<O>()` is `under` at `WithOverflow<S, O>`, and `with_rounding::<R>()` at
`WithRounding<S, R>`. These are how one site overrides one coordinate:
`a.with_overflow::<Saturate>().add(b.with_overflow::<Saturate>())` is the saturating sum of two values
held under a wrapping signature. Each names the sibling's admission in its own where clause. They suit
a site that names its signature. Code generic over the signature takes the target as a parameter and
calls `under`, because a bound over a projected sibling has to be written again by every generic caller
above it.

## The exact accumulator

The signature's `add` completes at every step. A long sum under saturation therefore depends on the
order it was taken in, and under either policy the result does not say whether the sum left the
range on the way. `Accumulator<S, T, CAP, C>` is the other way to sum: it holds the exact sum of at
most `CAP` members of `S`, in a value of a second signature `T`, and comes back to a value of `S`
once, through `S`'s adaptation.

`T` is named by the consumer and checked by the build. Two things are asked of it, in one constant
that every function forces. Its format sits on `S`'s grid, the same radix and the same quantum
exponent, so a member of `S` has the same slot index in `T`. And its range holds `CAP` times each end
of `S`'s range, so no sum of `CAP` members leaves it. Where either fails the accumulator is refused
as a failed constant. A width written by hand is then sufficient for the declared capacity or does
not compile: an 8-bit unsigned accumulator over 4-bit unsigned terms is admitted at a capacity of
sixteen, where the largest sum is 240, and refused at thirty-two, where it is 480. That pair is
`probe::a_runtime_length_fold_compiles_at_every_length_and_computes_at_one` with its second arm
turned into a refusal.

`T` is a function of `S` and `CAP` and is still written out. Deriving it would be arithmetic on a
const parameter in a type position, which the language does not have on stable, so the same fact is
stated as a check, and the width in the consumer's line is one the build has confirmed. The
accumulator has one type for the whole of a fold, which is what
`retirement::dl_growing_an_accumulator_type_per_iteration` leaves standing.

The capacity bounds how many terms have gone in, and the functions that put terms in keep it by
their shapes. Nothing is counted at run time.

1. `empty()` is the sum of no terms.
2. `of::<N>(terms)` takes an array of `N` terms and is refused at build where `N` is above `CAP`.
3. `take(terms)` takes a slice of any length, sums its first `CAP` terms, or all of them where it is
   shorter, and returns the accumulator with the rest of the slice. A slice longer than the capacity
   is summed a block at a time, each block exact, and the consumer says what joins the blocks: the
   signature's `add` on the finished values, or `plus` into a wider accumulator. The split is the
   operation, and nothing is validated.
4. `plus(a, b)` joins accumulators of capacities `A` and `B` over the same `S` and `T` into one of
   capacity `CAP`, refused at build where `A + B` is above `CAP`.
5. `minus(a, b)` is the exact difference of two such, as a value of `T`, with its own bound checked
   at build: `T` has to hold `A` times one end of `S`'s range less `B` times the other, which for
   unsigned terms is below zero. It gives a value and not an accumulator because a difference is not
   a sum of members: over unsigned terms it lies outside what any capacity bounds, so nothing more
   may be added to it under a capacity's name. `convert` takes it onto `S`.
6. `total()` is the exact sum as a value of `T`.
7. `finish()` is the sum as a value of `S`, completed by `S`'s overflow policy where it has left
   `S`'s range. It is `convert` from `T` onto `S`, and the grids being one, nothing rounds. Under the
   `Abort` check a debug build aborts where the completion would act.
8. `fits()` returns `Bool`, whether `finish` needs no completion.

A term enters by its slot index and the sum is `T`'s carrier arithmetic, which cannot leave `T`'s
range by the bound. So `T`'s rounding and overflow never act, and any adaptation `Sole` admits does
for it. The result of `finish` is a function of the multiset of terms: the exact sum has no order,
and the adaptation is applied to it once.

`probe::the_accumulator_width_is_the_exact_sum_width_less_one_bit` measures that a signed saturating
fold can do with one bit less than the exact sum needs. That bit is not taken. The contract here is
exactness, which `total`, `minus` and a wrapping `finish` all read, and the saved bit holds in one
cell.

An accumulator is a value of `T`, so it is at most 64 bits wide. A 64-bit term is admitted at a
capacity of one and no further, and a wider accumulator needs a carrier the tree does not have.

This is one carrier a chain of operations may use. Which carrier ships for chains is
`question::which_chain_carrier_ships`, open, and nothing here answers it.

## Aliases

`Uint<BITS, A, C>` is a value of `UFixed<BITS, 0>` and `Int<BITS, A, C>` of `Integer<BITS>`, and
`USize<A, C>` and `ISize<A, C>` are values of `arvo_format::points::USize` and
`arvo_format::points::ISize`. `Fix<BITS, FRAC, A, C>` is a value of `Fixed<BITS, FRAC>` and
`UFix<BITS, FRAC, A, C>` of `UFixed<BITS, FRAC>`, the fixed-point points, with `FRAC` the quantum's
exponent. The const parameters are bare integers and `C` defaults to `Abort`.

The fixed-point aliases are what a consumer with a deterministic lane writes, and the rounding is
its own to declare: `Fix<32, -16, Adapt<HalfEven, Saturate>>` rounds a product's tie to the even
neighbour because that line says so, and no alias fixes a mode. The product, the quotient and the
conversion round by the declared mode, and every operation is integer arithmetic on the declared
width, with no float and no step that reads the host, so the same line computes the same members on
every target. What such a consumer has to know about order is the format's to say and is repeated
here because it decides how a sum may be split. Under wrapping the sum is associative and any order
gives the same member. Under saturation over a signed range it is not, which
`law::additive_associativity_under_saturation` counts. A consumer that splits a saturating sum
declares its order, or takes the accumulator, which has none.

`A` is what names the adaptation, read through `NamesAdaptation<F>` at the alias's own format, and it
has no default. Which adaptation the default strategy names for integers, wrapping or saturation, is
held open by `ruling::wrap_or_clamp_stays_open_and_both_get_priced` until both are priced, so an alias
names its adaptation for now, `Int<8, Adapt<TowardZero, Wrap>>`. Every `Adapt<R, O>` names itself, for
every format.

`NamesAdaptation` takes the format because what a default names may depend on it. A preset that wraps
integers and saturates fixed-point quantities is one type naming two adaptations, and it says so with
one impl per region, or with a single blanket impl where it names one adaptation throughout. Whether
the shipped default has one region or several is `question::does_warm_wrap_or_clamp`'s to say, with
`question::why_the_default_rounding_position_is_chosen` for the rounding half, and a projection with
no format in it could state only the first answer.

The projection is what lets the strategy default arrive later without breaking a consumer.
`PRINCIPLES.md` has every semantic alias carry a default for its strategy parameter, and that default
`Warm`. Once a strategy's adaptation is claimed, the strategy implements `NamesAdaptation<F>` by its
own selection, over the formats it selects for, and `A` gains `= Warm`: a consumer that wrote `Adapt<R, O>` meant the adaptation
itself and still gets it, and `Int<8>` becomes legal where it is not today. The sketch
`202611130550-m67x_an-alias-that-takes-a-strategy-later` builds one consumer's source against both
versions of the alias, unchanged, and shows a consumer written against an alias without the
projection refused by the later one, which is why the projection ships now.
The impl is written for `Adapt<R, O>` and not as a blanket over every adaptation, so a strategy marker's
impl overlaps nothing. The sketch builds the value crate depending on the strategy crate, and that edge
is the one the design keeps open for it.

The host doors are written on the value type, `Num<Signature<points::USize, Ad>, C>`, rather than on the
alias, so they hold whatever names the adaptation.

`Bool` is `arvo_format::Bool`, re-exported, and not a value over a declared signature. It is the type
every predicate and verdict returns, the format's and this crate's alike: `eq`, `lt`, `fits_add` and the
format's own `panic_on_overflow` all give it. A boolean over a one-bit value would be a second type for
the same thing, and none of those would return it. A one-bit value's sum is also not how a yes or no is
read: under wrapping it is exclusive or, under saturation it is or. So a consumer finds the boolean
beside the numerals, and it is the one the stack already answers in.

## What is proved, and how

The suite is split into one test binary per width band, 1 to 8, 9 to 16, 17 to 32 and 33 to 64, so a
band's run is its own and the narrow bands stay quick.

Widths 1 to 8 are exhaustive: every signature over both slot families, both overflow policies under the
`Quiet` check and all five modes, every pair of members, through the value type and checked
against the oracle. The same widths are computed in each of the four rungs, which is the derived carrier
checked against every wider one, the 64-bit carrier among them. Widths 9 to 12 are computed in every
rung wide enough to carry them, the 16, 32 and 64-bit ones, both slot families, both policies, every
pair, against the oracle.

So the rungs are exhaustive at widths 1 to 12, which covers the 8-bit carrier at its full width and the
wider carriers below theirs, and the value type is exhaustive at 1 to 8. Widths 13 to 64 are sampled,
and every full-width branch of the 16, 32 and 64-bit carriers, the machine's own wrapping or saturating
add, falls among them: at width 16 alone there are four billion pairs per family and policy.
One fixed-point signature at a non-zero exponent, `UFixed<6, 3>`, is exhaustive the same way through the
value type: unsigned, as `UFixed` is, both policies, all five modes, every pair, against the oracle.
The exponent moves no slot of a sum, so this is the test that the sum reads the slot and never the
position. Its width sits below its 8-bit carrier's, so the rung's adaptation acts on every sum that
leaves the range and the 8-bit mutant turns it red; at the carrier's full width the adaptation is the
machine's own and no mutant of it could.

The difference and the product are proved at every place the sum is, on the same pairs, against the
format's `sub` and `mul`, and the negation on every member against `neg`: through the value type at
widths 1 to 8, in every rung at widths 1 to 8 and in the three wide rungs at 9 to 12, on the edge
lattice and the seeded pairs above that. The rung product is asked only at exponent zero, which is the
only place it is called. The unsigned 64-bit integer is left out of the product's pairs, since its
product is refused, and is the compile-fail case below.

The product is where the exponent does move the slot, so the fixed-point product has a test of its
own: `Fixed<6, -3>` and `UFixed<6, -3>`, every pair, all five modes and both policies, against the
oracle, and the same at the positive exponent of `UFixed<6, 3>`. One pair is named in each mode, the
members at slots `-3` and `4` of the signed format, whose product sits at minus one and a half slots:
a tie below zero, which goes to `-1` toward zero, on the ceiling and under `half_up`, and to `-2` on
the floor and under `half_even`.

Widths 9 to 64 run through the value type on an edge lattice, each end of the range and its neighbours,
zero and its neighbours, and every single-bit value, against the oracle, plus pairs drawn from a seeded
generator whose seed is committed in the test.

`fits_add`, `fits_sub`, `fits_neg` and `fits_mul` are checked against `panic_on_overflow` of the
format's own positions on the same pairs, `slot` returns the member a value was built from, and carrier
equality is slot equality.

The debug check is observed where it acts, `div`, `convert`, `from_index` and the accumulator's `finish` among the sites. One test per site, `add` and `adapted`, and per policy,
`Wrap` and `Saturate`, gives the site an input that leaves the range under `Abort`, left to the default
under `Wrap` and named under `Saturate`, and expects the site's own message; the abort is that check's
specified behaviour in a debug build, so these are `#[should_panic]` tests. Each has a twin under `Quiet` taking
the same input and completing it by the policy, one test shows `Abort` stays quiet on what fits, and
one that the two checks hold the same member in the same size. The binary is compiled only under
`debug_assertions`, since a release build has no abort to observe. Its release twin, compiled only without
`debug_assertions` and run by `cargo test --release`, takes the same inputs under `Abort` and shows them
completed by the policy, since the check is a constant branch that clears there.

One mutant per rung, behind the committed features `mutant-8`, `mutant-16`, `mutant-32` and `mutant-64`,
removes the rung's adaptation. The tests assert agreement with the oracle under every build and expect
nothing of a mutant, so each mutant turns red the tests that run through its rung and leaves the others
green. The red output of each mutant's run is committed.

One mutant per operation beside those, each the place that operation goes wrong first. `mutant-sub`
pins the unsigned saturating difference below zero to the top of the range where the format gives
zero. `mutant-neg` leaves the lowest signed member where it is under saturation at the carrier's
width, where the format gives the highest. `mutant-mul` pins a saturating product that leaves the
machine integer to the top whatever its sign. Each turns its own operation's tests red and leaves the
other operations green, and each red run is committed.

The crossing is exhaustive through the value type at widths 1 to 8 over both slot families: for every
ordered pair of the two policies and every ordered pair of the five modes, every member crosses to the
same slot, equals the value the three public doors build under the target, and crosses back to the
value it left. One case is named, the top of an unsigned 8-bit range and one, whose sum is zero under
the wrapping signature and the top under the saturating one it crosses into. `mutant-under` sends the
top of the range to the bottom in a crossing, turns these red and leaves every operation's tests green,
and its red run is committed.

The quotient is proved through the value type against the format's `div`: at widths 1 to 4 for every
member divisor but zero, and at widths 5 to 8 for a named set of divisors, both ends of the range,
one, minus one where the range has it, and an odd one, over both slot families, both policies, the
five modes and every dividend. `Fixed<6, -3>` and `UFixed<6, -3>` are proved the same way, where the
quotient's position falls between slots and the mode decides. `fits_div` is checked against
`panic_on_overflow` of `quotient_position` on the same pairs.

The conversion is proved against the format's `convert` on every member of each source: between
integer widths in both directions, across the two slot families, from an integer onto a fixed-point
grid and back, and between two fixed-point grids of different exponents, under both policies and the
five modes of the target. `fits_convert` is checked against `panic_on_overflow` of
`converted_position`, and a conversion between two signatures over one format equals `under`.

The host door is proved against `adapted`: at widths 1 to 8, both families and both policies, for
every `i8` and `u8`, every `i16` and `u16` within 1024 of zero, and the ends of every host integer; at
the wider widths on the ends of the range, their neighbours, and the ends of each host integer.
`holds_index` is checked against `panic_on_overflow` on the same inputs, and `index` returns what
`from_index` was given for every member, in every host integer the range fits.

`mutant-div` returns the dividend where the quotient leaves the range, which is what the machine's
wrapping quotient gives for the lowest signed member over minus one. `mutant-convert` reads the
source's slot index as the target's and so drops the two exponents. `mutant-index` makes the saturating door
send a negative host integer to the top of an unsigned range, which is what a comparison done after a
cast gives. Each turns its own tests red and leaves the others green, and each red run is committed.

The fixed-point aliases are checked to be the written types. One product is named through `Fix`: a
half times three eighths, at eighths, sits at one and a half slots, and is slot 2 under `HalfEven`
and slot 1 under `Floor`. A preset names an adaptation for a fixed-point format and the alias reads
it.

The accumulator is proved against the exact sum, computed in the test's own wide integers. Every
array of three terms at widths 1 to 4 and every array of two at width 5, both slot families, both
policies of `S`: `total` is the exact sum, `finish` its adaptation by the format's `adapt`, and
`fits` the format's verdict. Seeded slices of up to 300 terms at widths 8, 13 and 32 the same way.
`take` is asked on slices shorter than the capacity, equal to it and longer, and returns the rest it
did not sum. `plus` and `minus` are checked against the exact sum and difference of their halves,
`minus` over unsigned terms into a signed `T` among them. One case is named on the signed saturating
cell: the terms 7, 7 and -8 at four bits finish at 6 in every order, where the signature's own `add`
gives -1 in one order and 6 in another. The probe's numbers are run through the real type: sixteen
terms of 15 in an 8-bit accumulator total 240, and thirty-two taken at a capacity of sixteen total
240 with sixteen terms handed back.

`mutant-accumulator` makes `take` ignore the capacity and sum the whole slice, which is the
hand-written fold. It turns the capacity tests red, with 224 where 240 and a rest are asked, and its
red run is committed.

The count has two test targets off one shared module, one per path, each built only under its
feature. Both read the lengths of lists of zero to four cells and the indices of positions in the
platform-sized integer, a byte, a signed integer and a fixed-point value, through code generic over
the signature and the check, and a count past the range under wrapping and under saturation. The const
target reads the same lengths as constants, one of them as an array's length, and names no feature of
the toolchain, which is the evidence that a consumer needs no gate. Under `Abort` a count past the
range aborts in a debug build on the plain path; on the const path it is a compile-fail pair, a list
of four cells in two bits beside one of three, and a second pair refuses a length in a format without
a one beside a format with one. `mutant-count` stops a count at one and turns both targets red, and
its red run is committed.

`law::additive_associativity_under_wrapping` and `law::additive_associativity_under_saturation` are
reproduced through the value type, with the registry's own counts.

Compile-fail pairs under `tests/ui/`, each beside a passing twin that differs in the offending token,
with the diagnostic committed: width 0 and 65, `constant` of a non-member, the additive identity on a
format without one, `add` across two signatures, a host integer where a value goes and a value where a
host integer goes, the field named from outside, `Clamp`, `Stochastic` and a phased format. For this
round's operations: `sub` across two signatures, `mul` on the unsigned 64-bit integer beside the same
product at 63 bits, and the multiplicative identity on a one-bit signed integer beside a two-bit one.
For the override: a value under `WithOverflow` naming `Clamp` beside one naming `Saturate`, a value
under `WithRounding` naming `Stochastic` beside one naming `HalfEven`, and a crossing to a signature
over another format beside one to a sibling.
For the host door: `index::<u8>()` on a signed 8-bit integer beside `index::<i8>()`, and on a 9-bit
unsigned integer beside `index::<u16>()`.
For the accumulator: 8 bits over 4-bit terms at a capacity of thirty-two beside sixteen, `of` with
more terms than the capacity beside one within it, `plus` past the capacity beside one within it,
and an accumulator on another grid beside one on the terms'.
The
admission is shown open from both sides: an outside slot family over part of its width's range is refused
beside one over the whole range, with its own row in the placement, which is held; an outside policy that
clamps is refused beside one that wraps, which is held; and an outside row in the placement that names
the 8-bit container for a 16-bit family and writes its own `SoleCarrier::ADMITTED` checking nothing is
refused at `Sole::ADMITTED`, beside the same disarmed row naming the 16-bit container, which is held. Beside them, the routes to the carrier's type:
`S::Repr` with `S: Sole`, a projection on `Sole` itself, and `Held` and `Machine` by their paths, each
beside a twin naming the slot instead.

A consumer is a crate of its own, outside this workspace, that depends on `arvo-numeral` with no feature
line and reaches every public item: each type, trait and alias, each associated item of `Sole` and
`DebugCheck`, each door and operation, the identity, the host doors of `USize` and `ISize`, and the
derived traits. It implements `DebugCheck` once and builds an alias through it. It declares a preset
of its own that implements `NamesAdaptation` for two formats and names a different adaptation for
each, and an alias of its own over `Uint` whose adaptation parameter defaults to that preset, which is
the whole of what shipping a default takes. It overrides one coordinate at a type, through
`WithOverflow`, and at a value, through `with_overflow`, `with_rounding` and `under`. A test builds and
runs it, and it asserts what it reads. It names no feature, so it does not reach the count, whose two
targets are written as a consumer would write them.

The disassembly of `add` at 13 bits in 16 and at 64 bits beside the host's `wrapping_add` is committed as
an ad hoc reading, and called that: the bench harness cannot yet commit the disassembly of a named
function.

## The old families this is the base for

The arvo hilavitkutin pins, at `1b2796e9`, held much more than this tree does, and all of it is to come
back on the current fundamentals. This crate is the base for the families that hold a number: the
facade's integer, fixed-point and float numerals, the platform-sized and count types of the old storage
crate, the numeric contracts (absolute value, reciprocal, square root, total order, the sign
predicates), widening and narrowing, and above those the capacity and tensor types, hashing, the
sparse and spectral numerics, the graph and the cost dynamic program. Only the integer and the
fixed-point points are here. None of the rest is back yet, and each returns as its own round, at the same strictness: `no_std`,
no allocation, everything in types and contracts, and a cargo feature only where it is purely opt in.

## What is not here

A capacity type that proves a slice's length, with which `take` would hand nothing back by its type,
is a later round's. An accumulator wider than a value and an accumulator of products are not here.
The remainder is not here because the format ships none, and a quotient by a value is not here because
the format has no divisor built from a slot of a range that holds the zero; each is the format's to
ship and has its ticket. The default itself is not here: no alias selects an
adaptation until the two open questions are answered, and the line that does lands with the answer. A
crossing between the two debug checks on one signature is not here either. The adaptation at the container's width, which would
take the fractional product, the quotient and the conversion off the format's wide step, is
`arvo-format`'s to write and is not here. A value at shared occupancy, packed beside others, is a different placement
and not this crate's. A door for a value's bits as bits, where a consumer needs one, is an encoding
stated over the declared width, the shape an exact-width container in `arvo-bits` has, and never the
carrier; none is here.
